            Theme 01 · Sea-ice observations
          </p>

          <h2>
            What can satellites tell us about the physical state
            of melting sea ice?
          </h2>

          <p class="question">
            The challenge:
          </p>

          <p>
            During summer melt, the surface conditions that matter most
            physically - melt ponds, wet snow, roughness, and evolving
            ice structure - also alter the microwave signal used to
            observe the ice.
          </p>

          <p>
            My doctoral research used polarimetric C- and L-band SAR
            to show that the two frequencies contain complementary
            information about surface and volume scattering during
            advanced melt. That gives us a path toward more physically
            meaningful sea-ice interpretation when a single sensor
            becomes ambiguous.
          </p>

